// bubbler profile: steam
//
// The client is a 32-bit i386 binary and its UI (steamwebhelper) is an
// X11/CEF client with no Wayland support, so this profile needs `x11`.
// `"host"` rather than the nested server bubbler starts by default: the
// client opens several windows and there is no window manager inside.
// The Arch wiki's own bwrap example carries an accuracy dispute over
// exactly that forwarding: an X11 client can keylog and inject into every
// other X11 client, Xwayland included. Measured: the client and
// steamwebhelper both come up as Xwayland clients here.
//
// `wayland` is not for the client, then, but for what it starts: an SDL3
// or a native-Wayland game, and gamescope run nested on this session
// rather than embedded on a card node (Arch wiki, Gamescope). It costs
// nothing beside `x11 "host"`, which already hands over the whole
// display, and what goes through it goes through bubbler's proxy and a
// security context, which X11 has no equivalent of.
//
// `dri` is the GPU everything here renders on, the 32-bit client
// included. Bare, not `kms=#true`: a game drawing into a window on this
// session's compositor is not the display controller, and `kms=#true`
// would leave the card sysfs readable on top. What bare `dri` binds
// anyway on the proprietary NVIDIA driver, `lint` notes. Gamescope wants
// a card node only as an embedded compositor.
//
// `gamepad` is the controllers, read through evdev. It binds /dev/input
// whole, so the keyboard and the mouse of this seat come with it
// (measured: the nodes readable inside are the ones udev gave this login
// an ACL on) — which `x11 "host"` above already concedes.
//
// Sound is `pulseaudio`. The grant binds $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pulse/native —
// the socket PipeWire's pulse server holds on a PipeWire host — and sets
// PULSE_SERVER to it, which is the path a libpulse client takes; the
// client's CEF layer and the runtime it downloads are libpulse clients.
// Bare, it is playback only. Not measured here, because the client
// fetches its own runtime on first run: if a game is silent, add
// `pipewire` beside it.
//   pulseaudio { microphone }
//     — in-game voice chat with other players. Every microphone and
//       line-in the session has becomes reachable, and capture from
//       them.
//
// The default filter covers i386 as well as x86_64, so the 32-bit client
// and its games are filtered rather than killed, and `modify_ldt` stays
// allowed for the 16-bit and Wine code that needs it. Measured: the
// client reaches "Running Steam" with the filter on.
//
// Never add `userns "disable"`: Proton and the Steam Linux Runtime nest
// their own bubblewrap (pressure-vessel ships srt-bwrap), which has to
// create a user namespace inside this one.
//
// No `portals`: that grant writes /.flatpak-info, and Steam's runtime
// reads it as "I am the unofficial Steam Flatpak" —
// steam-runtime-check-requirements then demands the flatpak-portal
// service and exits 71, so steam.sh stops before the client starts
// (measured). Steam brings its own file browser; a path outside the
// private home is shared below instead.
//
// A first run is a download of the whole client — 496 MB, at whatever
// the link gives — with no window at all while it happens: the
// bootstrapper takes its console update UI while the installed client
// version is 0 ("Using update UI: console" in
// ~/.local/share/Steam/logs), and only an installed client's updater
// draws one. Measured past that point: the client, the Steam Linux
// Runtime and steamwebhelper all start, and the window is the sign-in
// one. steamwebhelper is where a broken sandbox shows first — it runs
// under pressure-vessel's own bubblewrap, which re-binds the /etc files
// this one has, and the client restarts it every ten seconds without
// ever saying why on its own stderr; its log is
// ~/.local/share/Steam/logs/webhelper-linux.txt.
//
// Steam builds its own ~/.steam and ~/.local/share/Steam in the private
// home on first run. The host's are not shared, because ~/.steam is a
// directory of absolute symlinks into the host home; move an existing
// install in instead, with the instance stopped:
//   mv ~/.local/share/Steam ~/.local/share/bubbler/instances/steam/home/.local/share/
// A library folder on another disk is named explicitly, e.g.
//   path-share "/kioxia/Steam" mode=rw
// Steam Input's virtual controllers need two more grants, each with a
// warning of its own in the README:
//   gamepad hidraw=#true uinput=#true
// The host side is multilib 32-bit GL and Vulkan drivers and an
// en_US.UTF-8 locale; the locale reaches the sandbox through LANG and the
// read-only /usr, with nothing to bind for it.
//
// `network` is the sandbox's own network namespace. Remote Play and local
// game discovery are LAN broadcast and pasta does not bridge, so neither
// crosses it; `network "host"` brings them back, and hands Steam every
// service on the host's loopback and every host abstract unix socket
// along with them.
//
// Bare by design. Add:
//   dbus {
//       own "com.steampowered.*"
//       talk "org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver"
//       talk "org.freedesktop.PowerManagement"
//   }
//   lint-allow {
//       "own-too-wide" reason="com.steampowered.* is Steam's own name space, and the client claims several names under it"
//   }
//     — the names the client claims for itself (steam:// URL handling
//       among them) and the two power-save blockers it holds while a game
//       runs.
//   system-bus {
//       talk "org.freedesktop.UPower"
//       see "org.freedesktop.UDisks2"
//       call "org.freedesktop.UDisks2=org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager.GetManagedObjects@/org/freedesktop/UDisks2"
//   }
//     — the power state the client reads from UPower, and the drive list
//       Wine builds from UDisks2: `see` plus that one call, and
//       no more. A `talk` there would reach loop setup, mount and LUKS
//       unlock, which polkit judges as you.
//   notify
//     — the friend and download notifications. Needs `dbus`.
//   tray
//     — the tray icon, and with it the client's "minimise to tray".
//       Needs `dbus`.
//   pipewire
//     — a game or the client speaking PipeWire itself, and the
//       screen-share portal, which `portals` above rules out anyway.
lint-allow "x11-without-reason" reason="steamwebhelper is an X11/CEF client with many windows; a nested x11 needs a wm= naming a window manager this host has"
wayland
x11 "host"
dri
pulseaudio
network
gamepad
command "steam"
